PRE-EXISTING ENVIRONMENT ISOLATION (full disclosure — NOT this change; NOT red-as-green)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The following PRE-EXISTING, out-of-scope files are UNMODIFIED by this change (verified via
`git status --porcelain` — none of them appear) and are outside this artifact's allowed files. They
fail identically on a clean tree under the available newer Xcode 26.5 / Swift 6.3 front-end (the
repo's macOS verification is documented PENDING in apps/macos-pending-verifications.md). Each was
isolated via the command-line build setting EXCLUDED_SOURCE_FILE_NAMES (a build override, NO source
edits) so this page's build + tests could be exercised:

  1) apps/apple/TeslaSync/shared-surfaces/Input.Previews.swift
     `cannot use explicit 'return' statement in the body of result builder 'ViewBuilder'` (×2) inside
     `#Preview` blocks — the ONLY app-target file with error diagnostics on a clean build.
  2) Tests/Unit/Features/Admin/GDPRExportPageModelTests.swift — main-actor isolation diagnostic
     (already disclosed in the Powershare P7 log).
  3) Tests/Unit/Features/Analytics/TimelinePageModelTests.swift — `call to main actor-isolated static
     method 'utcDayKey' in a synchronous nonisolated context`.
  4) Tests/Unit/Features/Admin/VehicleCostPageModelTests.swift — `main actor-isolated default value in
     a nonisolated context` (a nested StubSource default referencing a @MainActor-isolated value).
  (3) and (4) are the same Swift-6.3 strict-concurrency class as (2); they pre-date and are unrelated
  to this page. With them isolated, the test target compiles and this page's 20 tests pass (above).
  No CostAnalysis / AppRoute / AppRouteParser / TeslaSyncApp file produced any diagnostic.

iOS ARCH NOTE: `-destination 'generic/platform=iOS Simulator'` additionally links x86_64, but the
vendored Shared.xcframework ships only an `ios-arm64-simulator` slice (no x86_64), so the x86_64 link
fails on pre-existing `FacadeError.o` (`Undefined symbols … _OBJC_CLASS_$_SharedApiError`). This is a
framework-packaging constraint, not a code issue — arm64 is the relevant simulator arch on this Apple
Silicon runner, so the iOS build targets arm64 (BUILD_IOS_EXIT=0 above). Code signing was disabled
(no development certificate on this runner) — an environment constraint, unrelated to the page.

